>
> I'm not so sure. There's a balance between levelling up and monster
> toughness that you have to balance. The extra 9-10 points of AC

It's not an extra 9-10 points of AC.  A normal character will get 3-4
buys anyway, usually like 5 points of AC, so the racket only gains you
like 5 points for a few levels until you come back and get them with
additional $.

> >Just playing it straight without any racketeering I'm usually XL 7 or
> >so with enough money for half the protection when I get to Minetown,
> >and it's not too long before I get the rest.
>
> But I still find trouble in the monsters you come across at XL7. At that
> point killer bees, rothes, Mordor and hill orcs are the norm. And they
> come in packs. At XL1 or XL2 those monsters don't get generated.

Bees are the only tough ones there, but even if you can't hit them
consistently E and kicking them works fine.  The others are pretty much
pushovers as long as you don't just get surrounded and try to melee
them.  Occasionally you have to turn around and find PR if you've been
unlucky.  Remember against bees that MC3 protects fairly high
percentage against "the poison was deadly" if they happen to hit you,
so you take steps (E, speed, pets) etc to stop them from hitting you
but the danger if they do isn't _that_ high.

> >Whereas if you stay at XL1 it's tough to get enough HP to avoid a
> >randomly generated wand of lightning, fire, cold, etc from being an
> >instadeath.  And the whole key to surviving the game is to eliminate
> >instadeaths mercilessly.
>
> Actually rolling boulder traps are much more likely.

Yet another problem.  I've just never been able to pull the racket off
more than 75% of the time or so, which makes it a clear loss.  I guess
if you could get it into the 95% range it'd be worth considering,
although the gains are small and temporary so it'd still be a tough
call.

> In fact not levelling up is really hard work. There have been a number
> of times I've gained XPs simply by bumping into a goblin, fox, or
> gnome zombie. You have to pattern to let your pet lead.

Levelling up on DL1 is sometimes tricky, and with the first 2-3 dungeon
levels being the only ones where food is an issue it's not always
feasible.  But getting those first 2-3 levels greatly helps with the
"random damage" instadeaths, and is my normally my first early-game
priority (followed by a few points of AC from
boots/helm/shield/cloak/etc, then MC3 and PR, then sleep
res/MR/reflection, with at least one "out" in the form of /fire,
/teleport, ?tele, /digging, etc coming around the PR/MC point in most
games).

>
> It's just another strategy. As I've pointed out I'm not too hung up on
> staying XL1. But until you get the extra AC, the lower your XL, the
> lesser level of monster is generated. Frankly a XL9 large cat or dog
> truly outmatches any random monster that the RNG will generate with th
> PC at XL1 or XL2.

But in my experience, the additional HP and firepower (PW, to hit,
skill, etc) your character gets _more_ than makes up for the modestly
more challenging monsters, so long as you're ruthless about finding
good equipment and play smart.  The real crossover point is when
L/golden N get manufactured; if you lack MR, that can be a bummer.

I really can't see a mrivan/anselmus/frown/etc tearing off 10
ascensions in a row with the racket.  While mere mortals can't hope for
those streaks, if they want to maximize their ascension odds it still
seems like a bad gamble to me.  Any time you keep yourself in a
situation where random events are more likely to kill you, you're
decreasing your victory odds.  Eliminate chance and give yourself the
time and tools to apply strategy.

Now, it's not a _horribly_ bad strategy (like, say, fountain quaffing),
and it's fun to do, and for pacifists it's probably the right call.
And there's no reason to play for maximal odds of winning all the time;
if you enjoy the racketeer conduct, having fun is the point of the
game.

But if you're trying to win, I think it's one of those things where you
lose track of how much time it costs in failed attempts because the
victories seem so sweet.



